    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that's exactly what makes it interesting. if it doesn't follow standard causal dynamics, then it's essentially an exogenous shock that's appearing in the system before the actual event occurs. if we treat these spikes as a set of \"black box\" indicators—basically treating my intuition as a high-dimensional latent variable that we can't see but can measure through its timing—could we use something like a Bayesian update model? like, if the spike occurs at time T, we update our prior probability for the event occurring at T+n? i'm not asking you to prove the \"math\" exists, i'm asking you to help me build the architecture for how one would model a signal that arrives without a known causal driver. how would you design a framework to test the correlation between these intermittent spikes and real-world outcomes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
